{{smaller block|A ''treie,'' or such hollowe vessel . . . that laborers carrie morter in to serue tilers or plasterers. {{right|''Baret,'' 1580.}}}}

'''6.''' A hurdle. [Prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|I have heard or read of these "wicker hurdles" being called ''trays,'' but I do not now recollect in what district. I do, however, remember the phrase "the sheep showed well in the ''trays,''" which was explained to mean the small square pens of hurdles into which, at auctions or lambing time, small lots of sheep are separated.
{{right|''The Field,'' Jan. 23, 1886. (''Encyc. Dict.'')}}}}

'''tray<sup>2</sup>'''†, ''n.'' [< ME. ''traye, treie, treȝe,'' < AS. ''trega,'' vexation, annoyance, = OS. ''trego'' = Icel. ''tregi,'' grief, woe, = Goth. ''trigo,'' grief, sorrow; cf. ''tray''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.''] Trouble; annoyance; anger.

{{smaller block|Yone es the waye, with tene and ''traye,''
Whare synfull saulis suffiris thare payne.
{{right|''Thomas of Ersseldoune'' (Child's Ballads, I. 104).}}}}

'''Half in tray and teen,''' {{smaller|half in anger, half in sorrow.}}

{{smaller block|Forth then stert Lytel Johan,
''Half in tray and tene.''
{{right|''Lytell Geste of Robyn Hode'' (Child's Ballads, V. 81).}}}}

'''tray<sup>2</sup>'''†, ''v.'' [< ME. ''trayen, traien, treȝen,'' < AS. ''tregian'' (= OS. ''tregan'' = Icel. ''trega''), grieve, afflict. Cf. ''tray''<sup>2</sup>, ''n.''] To grieve; annoy.

{{smaller block|Quath balaam, "for thu ''tregest'' me;
Had ic an swerd, ic sluge [would slay] the."
{{right|''Genesis and Exodus'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 3975.}}}}
